"Simple Rankine cycle: water/steam, states 1 to 4

 A first look at the DeltaF Studio language. Equations can be written in any order and on either
 side of the '=' sign: DeltaF Studio works out which equation gives which variable (see Equations >
 Dependencies). Text in double quotes or braces is a comment; '//' starts a line comment.

 Also in this project:
   Cycle diagram   the flowsheet, with each stream's T, P, h and mass flow from the solution
   Parametric      efficiency over boiler pressure and turbine inlet temperature
   Plots           T-s diagram of the cycle, and the parametric results
   Goal seek       boiler pressure for a turbine exhaust quality of 0.90"

"The unit system of the model: C, bar, kJ, kg/s. A literal with a unit, like 10 [bar], is converted to it."
$UnitSystem Eng

"---------------------------------------------------------------- inputs"
P[1] = 0.1 [bar];  x[1] = 0    // condenser: saturated liquid at 0.1 bar
P[2] = 150 [bar]               // boiler pressure (pump outlet)
T[3] = 550 [C]                 // turbine inlet temperature
eta_p = 0.85                   "pump isentropic efficiency"
eta_t = 0.88                   "turbine isentropic efficiency"
m_dot = 50 [kg/s]              "steam mass flow"

"Pressure losses are neglected: the boiler and the condenser work at constant pressure"
P[3] = P[2]
P[4] = P[1]

"One mass flow for every state. Duplicate writes the equation once per index: m[1] = m_dot,
 m[2] = m_dot, ... The cycle diagram reads m[i] to show the flow of stream i."
Duplicate i = 1, 4
  m[i] = m_dot
End

"---------------------------------------------------------------- states
 Property functions take the fluid and two independent inputs by name (T, P, h, s, x, ...).
 Water is IAPWS-95 from CoolProp."
h[1] = enthalpy(Water, P=P[1], x=x[1])
s[1] = entropy(Water, P=P[1], x=x[1])
T[1] = temperature(Water, P=P[1], h=h[1])

"Pump 1 -> 2: the isentropic efficiency defines h[2]; DeltaF Studio solves it for h[2] by itself"
h_s[2] = enthalpy(Water, P=P[2], s=s[1])
eta_p = (h_s[2] - h[1]) / (h[2] - h[1])
T[2] = temperature(Water, P=P[2], h=h[2])
s[2] = entropy(Water, P=P[2], h=h[2])

"Turbine 3 -> 4"
h[3] = enthalpy(Water, P=P[3], T=T[3])
s[3] = entropy(Water, P=P[3], T=T[3])
h_s[4] = enthalpy(Water, P=P[4], s=s[3])
eta_t = (h[3] - h[4]) / (h[3] - h_s[4])
x[4] = quality(Water, P=P[4], h=h[4])
T[4] = temperature(Water, P=P[4], h=h[4])
s[4] = entropy(Water, P=P[4], h=h[4])

"---------------------------------------------------------------- energy balances
 A {unit} comment after an equation sets the unit the variable is shown in."
W_t = m[3]*(h[3] - h[4])       {kW}
W_p = m[1]*(h[2] - h[1])       {kW}
Q_in = m[2]*(h[3] - h[2])      {kW}
Q_out = m[4]*(h[4] - h[1])     {kW}
W_net = W_t - W_p              {MW}
eta_th = W_net/Q_in            "thermal efficiency"
bwr = W_p/W_t                  "back-work ratio"
check = Q_in - Q_out - W_net   "first-law check: zero to round-off"
